  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday night through Thursday: A ridging pattern remains in\nplace over the region, keeping mostly clear skies, light winds,\nand a subtle warming trend in the forecast through Thursday.\n\nLooking at temperatures, afternoon highs will generally range\nthroughout the 70s to around 80 degrees, around 10 to 15 \ndegrees above climatological normals for early October. Despite \nthe warm daytime temperatures, overnight/early morning lows will\nstill be chilly as is typical during the autumn months as the\nnights get longer. Strong radiational cooling under clear skies\nand light winds will support low temperatures dropping into the\nmid to upper 30s for the sheltered northern valleys. Patchy fog\nand frost has been observed in these valleys the past several \nmornings and will likely recur each morning through Thursday. \nFor most locations outside of the sheltered northern valleys, \nlows will be in the 40s. A few readings in the 50s are forecast\nat the foothills of the Cascades and Blue Mountains, where\ndownsloping winds will keep nighttime temperatures warmer.\n\nFriday through Monday: Confidence is increasing in the forecast\nfor Friday into early next week. 70 percent of ensembles now\nshow a trough dropping down the coast of British Columbia and\nover the Inland Northwest Friday afternoon into Saturday. As the\ntrough begins moving in from the north and tightening the\npressure gradient across the region, winds will pick up out of\nthe west. Sustained speeds will range from 15 to 25 mph for most\nlocations with the highest winds through the Cascade gaps and across\nthe Columbia Basin eastward into the Spokane area and the\nPalouse. Wind gusts have trended stronger in latest model runs\nwith widespread gusts of 35-40 mph in the forecast. \n\nIn addition to gusty winds, Friday's weather system will usher \nin significantly cooler temperatures. Afternoon highs on Friday \nwill be in the upper 60s to upper 70s. By Saturday, highs will \ndecrease by 10-20 degrees, topping out in the 50s for most \nareas. Highs remain in the 50s Sunday and Monday.\nOvernight/early morning lows will be noticeably cooler as well.\nFriday morning will see lows in the 40s to low 50s - similar to\nwhat we've been seeing the past few days. Saturday morning will\nfeel much chillier in the 30s to low 40s.\n\nLastly, the system will bring returning chances for\nprecipitation. Initially, strong westerly flow will result in a\nprominent rain shadow over much of Central and Eastern WA,\nkeeping precipitation focused over the Cascade Crest and ID\nPanhandle mountains. As the system shifts further south, \nprecipitation will wrap around the low, bringing precipitation \nchances to the northern mountains and east slopes of the \nCascades Saturday into early Sunday. By late Saturday night,\nsome of this precipitation could fall in the form of snow as\nsnow levels drop to around 4000 feet.\n\nHeading into Sunday, the trough continues its southward trek \ninto Oregon and Northern California, shifting moisture and\nprecip chances out of the region. Winds, however, will continue.\nHigh pressure to our north and low pressure to our south will\ncreate a north-to-south pressure gradient, resulting in another\nround of gusty winds out of the north Saturday night into \nSunday. Strongest winds are expected down the Okanogan Valley\nonto the Waterville Plateau and into the Columbia Basin as well\nas down the Purcell Trench. Expect sustained speeds around 20\nmph and gusts of 35-40 mph for the aforementioned areas.\n\nOverall, we're about to enter a much more fall-like pattern. The\nseasons are changing! /Fewkes",
